    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "The kettle"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A kettle on a flame warms the water until it boils. Heat is energy that flows because of a temperature difference."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "heat and temperature",
          "K = C + 273",
          "conduction, convection, radiation",
          "Q = m c delta T",
          "water 4200",
          "latent heat",
          "expansion"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "Temperature measures how hot a body is; heat is the energy transferred between bodies because of a temperature difference.\nTemperature is measured in degrees Celsius (C) or kelvin (K): K = C + 273.\nHeat moves by conduction (in solids), convection (in liquids and gases) and radiation (no medium needed).\nSpecific heat capacity c is the heat needed to raise 1 kg of a substance by 1 K; the heat required is Q = m c delta T.\nWater has a high specific heat, about 4200 J/kg K, so it heats and cools slowly.\nLatent heat is absorbed or released during change of state at constant temperature; melting, boiling and freezing are examples.\nMost substances expand on heating; water is unusual between 0 and 4 degrees Celsius."
        }
      },
